16. One more important optimization

For a large microservice landscape, I would eventually change the candidate index from just:

field count

to:

field count
+
field-name fingerprint
+
field-type fingerprint

For example:

AddressRequest
4 fields
addressLine1:String
city:String
state:String
zipCode:String

gets a fingerprint such as:

4|addressLine1|city|state|zipCode

Then we don't compare that DTO against hundreds/thousands of unrelated DTOs.

That becomes important if you're comparing entire enterprise repositories rather than two small services.

My recommended production flow

Java Files
    ↓
Parallel parsing
    ↓
DTO extraction
    ↓
Normalization
    ↓
Fingerprint/index
    ↓
Candidate filtering
    ↓
Parallel similarity calculation
    ↓
Best-match selection
    ↓
CSV + Excel

This gives you the speed of multithreading without blindly doing every DTO × every DTO comparison.

One caveat: the current implementation treats addressLine1:String and street:String as different fields. That's intentional. Automatically assuming those are equivalent can produce bad commonization recommendations. A next-level version can add a configurable semantic field-name mapping (e.g. zipCode, postalCode, zip) so those can be flagged as possible semantic matches rather than incorrectly classified as exact matches.